# Exocortex v1 — Round 6 Whole-Project Review

**Status:** OPEN — changes requested.

**Review baseline:** `f7bf463aa4a77ac719c485bfe713541d79edeab1`
(2026-08-27). Scope was the entire tracked repository: 199 files, of which
184 were classified as hand-authored, 14 as generated/copied artifacts, and
one as a lockfile. This review supersedes any claim that the post-BR-PRD tree
is release-ready. Findings below remain open until the master plan records
closing evidence and a fresh strict whole-project review finds no verified
actionable issue.

**Method:** nine independent lenses covered correctness, security, tests,
migrations/persistence, performance, architecture, product/PRD conformance,
cleanup, and cross-boundary hazards. Every accepted finding was reopened and
verified against the baseline before inclusion. The review was read-only and
made no repository or GitHub changes.

## Release verdict

**Changes requested.** Thirty verified blockers affect authentication,
visibility, durability, fencing, rollback, backend parity, resource bounds,
release integrity, or formal acceptance coverage. Eight advisory findings are
also accepted work. Seven items remain explicit PRD-backed deferrals. Four
questions require targeted verification before they may be closed or promoted
to defects.

Closure rules:

1. Every behavioral fix has a regression test that fails without the fix.
2. Gates must enforce the stated contract; a renamed, narrowed, or skipped
   test is not closure evidence.
3. A PRD conflict is recorded in `docs/MILESTONE_REPORT.md`, never silently
   resolved by changing behavior or weakening the contract.
4. Each item is closed in `docs/master-plan.prd` with concrete test/gate or
   commit evidence in the same commit as its fix.
5. A fresh strict whole-project review must return zero verified actionable
   findings before release.

## Blocking findings

### Security and authorization

#### R6-B01 — Release configurations contain a public signing key

The cluster/ingest release path can use `[0x42; 32]`, a value published in the
source. A deployed node can therefore appear configured while accepting
signatures computable by anyone. Production/shared mode must fail closed when
the secret is absent or malformed. Explicit local test/dev setup may inject a
known key but must not be reachable accidentally from a release invocation.

#### R6-B02 — Project and team membership are not enforced end to end

Visibility context carries project/team dimensions, but backend construction
and request paths discard or fail to enforce them. Reads and writes must apply
the authenticated principal's membership at every storage, cache, operation,
and transport boundary. Tests must cover same-org users with disjoint project
and team membership.

#### R6-B03 — SSE publishes rows outside subscriber visibility

Bearer authentication alone does not authorize every event in the change
feed. Filter events using the same visibility semantics as ordinary reads,
including replay. A subscriber must neither receive payloads nor infer hidden
row identifiers through event metadata.

#### R6-B04 — Producers can select their own production ceiling

The ingest type exposes administrator ceilings but production assembly does
not provision them, leaving the producer-provided value authoritative in the
deployed path. Production startup must load an administrator-owned policy and
fail closed for missing/unknown producer policy. Producer registration must
never create or widen that policy.

#### R6-B05 — `accept_discovery` accepts inventable or unscoped proposals

Acceptance is not strongly bound to a server-issued proposal, region, caller,
and proposed visibility, allowing fabricated identifiers or widened scope.
Persist an immutable proposal record/token and require exact scoped acceptance;
the accepted result may not exceed proposal or caller visibility.

#### R6-B19 — Shared-mode HTTP defaults and docs are plaintext

Public shared-mode configuration and documentation must not normalize bearer
tokens or graph data over plaintext HTTP. Require TLS/secure transport, or an
explicitly named local-only mode bound to loopback with prominent constraints.

#### R6-B25 — MCP inputs lack bounded resource contracts

Add and enforce limits for request bytes, memory content, tags, and edges
before expensive decode/allocation/work. Rejections must be deterministic and
tested at boundary, just-under, and just-over values.

#### R6-B26 — Metrics expose identities and unbounded label cardinality

Protect metrics consistently with the deployment threat model. Remove raw or
free-form user/org/producer identity labels, or map them to a bounded safe
dimension. Tests/gates must reject new unbounded label sources.

### Cache, visibility, and reasoning correctness

#### R6-B06 — Backend clients never establish continuous cache sync

The backend client cache starts empty and production does not run the SSE sync
loop. Hydrate before readiness, subscribe with replay, reconcile LSN gaps, and
recover from stream/fetch failures without serving a permanently stale cache.

#### R6-B07 — Derived relationships are hardcoded to org visibility

Reasoning writeback stamps derived edges as organization-visible rather than
deriving the narrowest authorized visibility from evidence/endpoints. Centralize
the visibility rule and test project/team/user evidence combinations.

#### R6-B13 — Traversal crosses invisible intermediate nodes

Filtering only returned nodes still allows hidden nodes to create paths between
visible endpoints. Apply visibility during expansion, not after traversal, and
test that an invisible bridge cannot affect reachability, distance, or ranking.

#### R6-B21 — The search arena retains update-history garbage

The cache search arena is append-oriented and grows with repeated replacements,
even when logical resident data is bounded. Reclaim/compact stale entries or use
a bounded index representation; include a repeated-update allocation/growth
regression test.

#### R6-B22 — Invalidations clone the graph and edge lookup scans all edges

Each invalidation performs graph-scale copying, and edge fetch paths linearly
scan all relationships. Introduce indexed/delta application while preserving
the lock-free read contract. Benchmark update and edge-fetch scaling, not only
steady-state search.

#### R6-B23 — Reasoning repeatedly scans the entire store

Incremental cycles re-read graph-wide state for work that should be bounded by
the changed region/frontier. Add indexed or delta-driven inputs and a scaling
test proving a small update does not cause a full-store scan.

### Atomicity, persistence, and backend parity

#### R6-B08 — Fence validation is not atomic with mutation

A lease may become stale after validation but before the write commits. Both
InMemory and Falkor implementations must make epoch comparison and mutation one
atomic operation, with a concurrency test where ownership changes at the
critical boundary.

#### R6-B09 — Deduplication races and persisted dedup is not production-wired

Concurrent requests can both pass the check before registration, while the
persisted registry builder is not used by production assembly. Make claim and
commit atomic, define retry/crash semantics, wire persistence in production,
and test concurrent duplicate submission plus restart.

#### R6-B10 — Dreams rollback corrupts pre-existing state

Rollback soft-deletes rows that were already closed before a failed cycle and
does not fully reverse relationships created by the cycle. Track the exact
write-set/preimage and restore it atomically. Test mixed pre-existing and newly
created memories/edges under injected mid-cycle failure.

#### R6-B12 — Dreams accepts nonexistent named project regions

Named regions can be scheduled/processed without validating that the project
exists and belongs to the org/caller. Reject missing or unauthorized region
keys before lease acquisition or mutation.

#### R6-B14 — Falkor `find_by_entity` queries data that writes never create

The adapter searches `Entity`/`MENTIONS` graph structures absent from its write
model. Align the registered Cypher catalogue with the canonical persisted
representation and add the same conformance case to the InMemory and live
Falkor suites.

#### R6-B15 — Corrupt WAL records are silently discarded

Deserialization/filtering treats malformed or newly incompatible records as if
they did not exist. Surface a precise corruption/incompatibility error with
record location while preserving the original file for recovery.

#### R6-B16 — WAL import is not all-or-nothing

Validation and append are separated such that a later error can leave an
imported prefix. Stage and atomically commit the complete import, with injected
failure tests proving the destination is byte/logically unchanged.

#### R6-B17 — Malformed persisted fingerprints are treated as absent

A malformed fingerprint can enter the initialization path and be overwritten
as though no fingerprint existed. Distinguish missing from malformed and fail
closed without mutating persisted state.

#### R6-B18 — Audit persistence is not atomic and errors are swallowed

Protected mutations may succeed without their required audit record. Define a
single atomic commit boundary (or a durable outbox with equivalent guarantees),
propagate failure, and test both mutation-side and audit-side injected faults.

#### R6-B20 — Backup creation is neither atomic nor permission-restricted

Write to a same-directory temporary file, flush/sync as required, set owner-only
permissions (for example `0600` on Unix), then atomically rename. Failure must
leave any previous backup intact.

### Runtime completeness, delivery, and release truth

#### R6-B11 — Production Dreams cycles never run discovery

The discovery implementation exists but the production cycle path does not call
it. Wire it at the specified lifecycle point, with deterministic budgets and a
production-path integration test.

#### R6-B24 — Ingestion is sequential N+1

The commit path performs per-row/per-edge storage work serially. Add bounded
batch operations or safe concurrency while preserving ordering, validation,
fencing, audit, and all-or-nothing semantics. Add a query/round-trip-count or
scaling regression test.

#### R6-B27 — CI omits seam gates and release lacks a correctness prerequisite

The documented gate list and actual CI/release dependency graph differ. Run all
mandatory gates on push/PR and require their successful aggregate before any
release/publish job can execute.

#### R6-B28 — Gates do not enforce their advertised contracts

Strengthen at least `storage-conformance`, `dead-enforcement`, `no-llm`,
`kernel-purity`, and `signing-hygiene`. Each must prove the semantic invariant,
include the promised targets/features, and fail under a deliberate violating
fixture. A grep that misses aliases, generated targets, or production callers
is not sufficient evidence.

#### R6-B29 — Publishing bypasses checks and can damage the worktree

`scripts/publish.sh` uses `--no-verify`, can destroy dirty manifest edits, and
can label a mixed-version release. Refuse dirty overlapping manifests, preserve
unrelated changes, run required verification, and derive/validate one coherent
release version before mutation or publication. Test the script in a disposable
repository fixture.

#### R6-B30 — Formal PRD §23 acceptance coverage is incomplete

Build a requirement-to-test/gate matrix for every §23 criterion. Implement all
non-deferred missing cases. Each deferral must have an explicit PRD citation and
master-plan row; prose claims and indirectly related tests do not count.

## Advisory findings

| ID | Accepted improvement | Closure evidence |
|----|----------------------|------------------|
| R6-A01 | Cache tests use fixed sleeps. Replace them with deterministic readiness/event synchronization. | No timing sleep used as correctness synchronization. |
| R6-A02 | Organization-backup round-trip asserts `relationships[0]` even though order is unspecified. Compare by identity/set or define canonical ordering. | Test passes under reordered relationship output. |
| R6-A03 | The SSE test named “within 500ms” waits 1000ms. Enforce the stated 500ms contract or rename/document an authoritative budget. | Name, deadline, and PRD agree. |
| R6-A04 | SLO/allocation gates cover too narrow a slice of production behavior. Add update, hydration, invalidation, and relevant backend-path budgets. | Gates exercise representative production paths. |
| R6-A05 | `exocortex-worker` contains a placeholder library surface. Implement the intended public contract or remove the unused target. | No shipped placeholder target/API. |
| R6-A06 | The entity interner is unused. Remove it or wire it with measured value and tests. | No dead interner state/code. |
| R6-A07 | Dead/no-op code obscures real enforcement. Remove it where behavior is unchanged. | Clippy/tests plus targeted review. |
| R6-A08 | Forty-four path-banner comments merely repeat filenames. Remove banners that carry no invariant or rationale. | Focused cleanup review. |

## Items requiring verification

These are not closed merely because the initial review could not prove them.
Investigate them with focused tests or primary backend documentation and record
one of: verified defect promoted to the backlog, verified-safe with evidence, or
explicit PRD-backed deferral.

| ID | Question |
|----|----------|
| R6-U01 | Does an empty configured bearer token create an authentication bypass or fail startup/requests closed on every transport? |
| R6-U02 | Do the exact Redis/Falkor `MULTI`/`EXEC` commands used here provide rollback/atomic failure semantics for all write errors, or can partial effects commit? |
| R6-U03 | Is the stored sled `Db` lifetime field required to keep trees valid, or is it dead ownership with hidden shutdown/flush consequences? |
| R6-U04 | Is `intern_name` a downstream public API contract? Determine this before removal or signature changes. |

