## PRD §23 disposition at review time

The review found criteria 4–12 and 16–17 met on the inspected baseline.
Criteria 1–3, 14–15, 18–21, 24–25, and 27–30 were not met or only partially
met. Criteria 13, 22, 23, and 26 were treated as explicit deferrals. R6-B30
must replace this coarse disposition with a line-by-line matrix citing the
exact executable evidence for every criterion.

## Validation observed on the baseline

Passed:

- `cargo fmt --all -- --check`
- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`
- `cargo test --workspace --features exocortex-adapter-sdk/testing --no-fail-fast`
  (235 passed, 2 ignored)
- `cargo deny check` (duplicate-version warnings only)
- `cargo xtask kernel-purity`
- `cargo xtask fingerprint` (`e1f7d17b…`)
- `cargo xtask gen-schemas`
- `cargo xtask gen-playbook` (299 words)
- `cargo xtask no-llm`
- `cargo xtask proto-sync`
- `cargo xtask signing-hygiene`
- `cargo xtask wire-standalone`
- `cargo xtask bench` (search p99 approximately 762µs; k-hop p99
  approximately 373µs)
- `cargo xtask write-path-parity`
- `cargo xtask dead-enforcement`
- `cargo xtask auth-coverage`
- `cargo xtask artifact-equivalence`

Failed or unavailable:

- `cargo xtask storage-conformance` failed because
  `org_round_trip_is_byte_faithful_across_storage_instances` assumes unordered
  relationships appear at index zero. The isolated test failed the same way.
- The gate attempts `cargo test --features integration -p exocortex-storage
  --test integration_live --no-run`, but the `integration_live` target does not
  exist. Restore a real live-Falkor conformance target.
- `FALKOR_URL` was unset, so no live Falkor suite ran.
- Docker/chaos validation did not run.

## Recommended implementation order

1. Fail-closed secrets/authentication and visibility enforcement (R6-B01–B05,
   B19, B25–B26).
2. Storage atomicity, fencing, rollback, WAL/backup durability, and adapter
   parity (R6-B08–B10, B12, B14–B18, B20).
3. Cache/SSE/reasoning correctness and production lifecycle wiring
   (R6-B06–B07, B11, B13, B21–B24).
4. CI, gates, publishing, and complete §23 traceability (R6-B27–B30).
5. Advisories and verified disposition of R6-U01–U04.
6. Full validation, then repeated strict whole-project review/remediation until
   no verified actionable finding remains.

## Fresh strict-review follow-up (2026-08-27)

A nine-lens strict review of current `main` after R6-R54–R6-R64 found additional
actionable work. The deduplicated findings are accepted as R6-R65–R6-R87 in the
master plan: invalidation-stream continuity; two missing live corruption/guard
regressions; installer origin trust and bearer strength; four semantic gate
bypasses; bounded legacy hydration; mixed-version invalidation delivery and
rollback history; production cache batching/recovery load; blocking embedding;
Dreams locality; client URL redaction; ingest admission ordering; and five
cleanup/structural corrections. Product-lens duplicates of mixed-version feed
and rollback defects remain owned by R6-R75/R6-R76.

The previously open new-client/old-server question is promoted as R6-R74. The
repository documents additive evolution and rollout work but no deployment
order that permits an indefinite pre-readiness wait; the runtime must therefore
either hydrate compatibly or fail promptly and explicitly. This is not closed
by assumption or by the D10 rollout-test deferral.

The mandatory full-workspace run then reproduced the historical SSE timing
flake in two tests even though the focused client suite passed. R6-R88 promotes
that verification result: keep the exact production deadlines, but serialize
the independent in-process network harnesses so sibling servers and reconnect
loops cannot consume one another's wall-clock budgets.

## Final strict-review remediation tranche (2026-08-27)

A fresh nine-lens strict review of `ad33946` found 24 additional actionable
items, promoted as R6-R89–R6-R112 in the master plan. The blocking set covers
cross-node mutation/LSN order, cluster-feed supervision and admission, HTTP
diagnostic containment, executable acceptance evidence, legacy tenancy and
rolling-writer repair, old-client feed progress, bounded/coalesced distributed
Dreams scheduling, explicit org configuration, governed backup restore,
durable discovery delivery, and crash-recoverable Dreams journals. Strict-mode
advisories cover reseed allocation, default storage ordering, quiet-hours
configuration, obsolete fire DTOs, registry lock scope, idle wakeups, and live
test synchronization. Duplicate reports of the dead cluster task and Redis
queue defects remain owned by R6-R90 and R6-R98/R6-R109 respectively.

Integrated remediation exposed one additional behavioral mismatch: the shared
Dreams API accepts batched memory and relationship deltas, but its local
fallback recorded every call as exactly one memory and zero relationships.
R6-R113 promotes the defect so the in-process and Redis transports retain the
same trigger accounting contract with executable regression evidence.

The live integration run then reproduced cross-test invalidation consumption:
unique graph names did not isolate the org-scoped Redis channel, so parallel
cross-node cases could count sibling commits. R6-R114 scopes both graph and org
per harness while retaining two peers inside each test.

Live governed-backup validation also exposed invalid harness evidence: the
Falkor driver rejects graph-entity decoding on a single-thread Tokio runtime,
and the test's row collector silently discarded that error as an empty graph.
R6-R115 runs the live test on the driver's required multithread runtime and
makes every stream error fail the regression.

The full workspace run then exposed hash-order dependence in the backup
fixture's ontology selection: it could choose a self-inverse or bidirectional
kind while asserting that storage materialized two rows. R6-R116 constrains
the fixture to a non-computed kind with a distinct inverse companion.

The mandatory dead-enforcement gate then correctly rejected its stale
singular Dreams witness after ingestion moved to exact batched deltas.
R6-R117 binds the gate to the production `on_writes` entrypoint instead of a
now-unused compatibility wrapper.

## Final convergence strict review (2026-08-28)

A fresh strict review of committed `1267fb9` against `origin/main` ran all nine
required lenses over the complete 161-file hand-authored/parity surface. The
preflight SQL-schema and container-test scanners emitted no leads; all three
hazard classes fired. After root verification and deduplication, the actionable
results are accepted as R6-R118–R6-R143 in the master plan.

The confirmed defects cover startup and reconnect loss windows; durable
post-ingest effects; mixed-version storage compare-and-set and assertion-shape
correctness; bounded regional Dreams reads; explicit audit authority; SSE
embedding and connection admission; executable acceptance evidence; standalone
runtime and release topology; immutable live-test inputs; backup fidelity and
idempotency; and two test-only production surfaces. Duplicate lens reports are
owned by the single corresponding plan row.

Three hazard questions received evidenced disposition. Backend URLs are
validated before credential attachment and reject userinfo, so the proposed URL
credential-to-log path is retired. Deployment or proxy connection limits are
not a substitute for first-party SSE admission and are owned by R6-R142.
Principal policy files contain plaintext bearer credentials and the loader did
not validate their mode, so that question is promoted as R6-R143 rather than
closed by deployment assumption.

The first complete workspace run after remediation promoted R6-R144. Five
targets still encoded the replaced contracts: Round-1 ingest tests did not run
the durable post-effect drainer; backend fixtures granted ordinary principals
implicit audit access; out-of-process fixtures wrote principal secrets with
ambient permissions; and the installed standalone test used a timing-sensitive
supervisor observation. These are harness defects, not permission to weaken the
new runtime controls.

R6-R144 is closed by executable lifecycle evidence. Both Round-1 effect tests
pass with the same durable drainer retained by backend assembly; the complete
backend-node target passes with audit permission granted explicitly; both
out-of-process node fixtures pass with owner-only credential files; and the
installed standalone supervisor/MCP test passes in five consecutive runs after
using a bounded observation window. No production authorization, policy-mode,
or durability check was weakened.

The subsequent mandatory `dead-enforcement` run promoted R6-R145: its Dreams
control manifest still named the pre-outbox `on_writes` call at the ingest
boundary. The production boundary now deliberately calls `on_writes_once` so
ambiguous durable-effect retries cannot double-count. The gate must track that
exact entrypoint and retain an executable manifest regression.

R6-R145 is closed: the control manifest now names `on_writes_once`, its unit
test rejects the superseded name, and `cargo xtask dead-enforcement` passes
against the executable ingest call graph.

The live cluster run promoted R6-R146. Direct `sse_router` integration
fixtures install a scoped principal extension but their raw HTTP requests
predate the handler's required bearer presence check, so reconnect correctly
returned 401. Both direct-router live requests must carry explicit credentials;
production authentication remains owned by the enclosing `HttpBind` middleware.

R6-R146 is closed without an authentication bypass: both direct-router
fixtures now send an explicit bearer header, and the live cluster matrix passes
the node-loop, cluster, cross-node reconnect, and SSE delivery targets.
